WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
River inputs, upwelling and remineralisation can supply nutrients.

00:08.000 --> 00:16.000
A nutrient pulse supports additional production only if organisms can use it under the prevailing light, temperature, delivery and loss conditions.

00:16.000 --> 00:21.000
Biomass can accumulate when production exceeds losses.

00:21.000 --> 00:25.000
Organic matter may settle and be respired by organisms below.

00:25.000 --> 00:30.000
The coastal scene is a conceptual sequence, not a forecast or the timing of the numerical experiments.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
Compare a control, nitrogen addition, phosphorus addition, and both nutrients.

00:35.000 --> 00:39.000
All start with the same biomass, light, carbon and other conditions.

00:39.000 --> 00:44.000
First predict the growth response.

00:44.000 --> 00:57.000
Each additional model biomass unit requires ten micromoles of nitrogen and one of phosphorus.

00:57.000 --> 01:11.000
Nitrogen alone increases the yield; phosphorus alone does not.

01:11.000 --> 01:24.000
Adding both increases it further as phosphorus restricts the nitrogen-enriched treatment.

01:24.000 --> 01:33.000
Maximum additional biomass is the smaller of nitrogen divided by ten and phosphorus divided by one in this fixed-composition model.

01:33.000 --> 01:43.000
Real composition and nutrient stores vary.

01:43.000 --> 01:52.000
Redfield and benthic-plant ratios are empirical summaries, not universal optimal requirements.

01:52.000 --> 01:57.000
Both dark bottom-water examples start with six milligrams of organic carbon per litre and eight milligrams of oxygen per litre.

01:57.000 --> 02:03.000
Change oxygen renewal alone.

02:03.000 --> 02:08.000
There is no photosynthesis in these bottom-water compartments.

02:08.000 --> 02:19.000
Aerobic decomposition consumes oxygen and regenerates ammonium nitrogen.

02:19.000 --> 02:30.000
Oxygen declines much further under weak renewal.

02:30.000 --> 02:41.000
Decomposition itself slows when oxygen becomes scarce.

02:41.000 --> 02:52.000
The model accounts for initial oxygen, added oxygen and consumed oxygen.

02:52.000 --> 03:01.000
Measure nutrient additions, biomass production and losses, organic matter, respiration, light and oxygen renewal.

03:01.000 --> 03:11.000
Nitrogen uptake is not the same as permanent nitrogen removal.

03:11.000 --> 03:20.000
Recycling can return acquired nitrogen to the water; harvesting or export would cross the chosen system boundary.
